APPENDIX
A LOWER BOUNDS AND OPTIMALITY OF
OUR ALGORITHMS
In this section, we analyze the complexity of the DAR problem and
show the near-optimality of our proposed algorithms. Specifically,
the well-studied half-space range counting problem [ 1,11] reduces
to our problem. Hence, the complexity of the state-of-the-art algo-
rithms for half-space range counting provides a lower bound for
theDAR problem.
The half-space range counting problem is defined as follows:
given a point set in R𝑑and a query half-space 𝐻, determine the
number of points lying within 𝐻.
Lemma 8. LetAbe an algorithm that solves the DAR problem
with query time 𝑇(A) and space complexity 𝑆(A). Then, usingA,
one can solve an instance of the half-space range counting problem
using space𝑆(A) and query time:
Θ(log𝑛·𝑇(A)).
□
Proof. To count the number of points within a given half-space
𝐻, we can perform a binary search over 𝑘∈{1,2,...,𝑛}. In each
iteration, we query Ato retrieve the 𝑘-th ranked point 𝑝(𝑘). If
𝑝(𝑘)∈𝐻, then the count of points in 𝐻is at least𝑘; otherwise, it isless. This process requires Θ(log𝑛)queries toA, resulting in total
query time Θ(log𝑛·𝑇(A)) . □
It is known that the query time for half-space range counting in
R𝑑with𝑚units of space satisfies the following lower bound [11]:
Ω(︃𝑛
𝑚1/𝑑log𝑛)︃
. (5)
Applying Lemma 8, this implies a lower bound for the DAR prob-
lem:
𝑇(A)· log𝑛≥𝑛
𝑚1/𝑑log𝑛=⇒𝑇(A)≥𝑛
𝑚1/𝑑log2𝑛.
Now, these are the two important observations:
Observation 1. If the algorithm uses linear space, i.e., 𝑚=𝑂(𝑛),
then the query time cannot be faster than 𝑂(𝑛1−1
𝑑)up to logarith-
mic factors.
Observation 2. If the algorithm uses exponential space, i.e., 𝑚=
2Θ(𝑑), then the lower bound becomes Ω(log𝑛). In fact, our exact
algorithm KthLevel has logarithmic query time but requires ex-
ponential space, which matches this bound. Note that querying
KthLevel𝑛times is equivalent to sorting all 𝑛elements, which
takesΩ(𝑛log𝑛)time.
Conclusion. These results demonstrate that our algorithms for
theDAR problem are optimal up to logarithmic factors, given the
space-time trade-offs imposed by known lower bounds.

